WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.339 --> 00:00:01.674
- The only reason I could
deal with the pressure

00:00:01.674 --> 00:00:04.499
on Star Wars is 'cause I
love making that movie.

00:00:04.499 --> 00:00:07.616
And if it didn't work, of
course I would have been

00:00:07.616 --> 00:00:11.589
crushed, but I wasn't doing
it because it was a job.

00:00:11.589 --> 00:00:13.833
But most people talk
about writing screenplays

00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:15.431
but don't actually write them.

00:00:15.431 --> 00:00:17.442
The people who write them,
you're already in like,

00:00:17.442 --> 00:00:19.520
the top 10% because you've
actually written it.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.212
10 years ago, if we wanted to do that,

00:00:21.212 --> 00:00:22.576
we'd have to kill a stunt man.

00:00:22.576 --> 00:00:26.591
We'd actually... (audience laughs)

00:00:26.591 --> 00:00:27.812
- Hello, believe nation.

00:00:27.812 --> 00:00:28.645
It's Evan.

00:00:28.645 --> 00:00:31.060
My one word is believe,
and I believe in people

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.322
more than they believe in
themselves, and my sincere hope

00:00:34.322 --> 00:00:37.623
is that if you see in
yourself what I see in you,

00:00:37.623 --> 00:00:39.561
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:39.561 --> 00:00:41.664
So to help you on your journey,
today we're going to learn

00:00:41.664 --> 00:00:44.510
from film director and
producer, JJ Abrams,

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.593
and my take on his top
10 rules for success.

00:00:47.593 --> 00:00:49.235
Rule number six is my personal favorite,

00:00:49.235 --> 00:00:51.641
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:51.641 --> 00:00:54.180
Also, as you're watching,
if you hear something

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.426
that really resonates
with you, please leave it

00:00:55.426 --> 00:00:57.184
down in the comments below,
and put quotes around it

00:00:57.184 --> 00:00:59.398
so other people can be
inspired by it as well.

00:00:59.398 --> 00:01:01.187
And if you leave it within
the first couple hours

00:01:01.187 --> 00:01:03.251
of this video going live,
you have a chance to win

00:01:03.251 --> 00:01:05.349
one of two daily prizes.

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:08.411
(swooshes)

00:01:08.411 --> 00:01:12.244
(sweeping instrumental music)

00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:16.417
(beeps)

00:01:18.353 --> 00:01:21.800
- Given all the
opportunities that are there

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.858
in the world, to try
and figure out the thing

00:01:23.858 --> 00:01:27.485
that actually makes you want
to get up in the morning,

00:01:27.485 --> 00:01:28.551
the thing that you actually love.

00:01:28.551 --> 00:01:32.718
I just know that if I
weren't lucky enough to have

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.144
been given the opportunity
to write a movie or a show

00:01:38.144 --> 00:01:39.626
or direct anything or produce anything,

00:01:39.626 --> 00:01:42.447
I would still go home from
whatever that day job is,

00:01:42.447 --> 00:01:45.617
and I would try to do
it because I love it,

00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:50.000
not because I really want to
make a certain amount of money.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.517
It's great to be comfortable.

00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:53.320
It can be dangerous to be comfortable.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:54.337
But it's great to be comfortable.

00:01:54.337 --> 00:01:57.898
But I just feel like if you
can figure out the thing

00:01:57.898 --> 00:02:00.348
that makes you tick.

00:02:00.348 --> 00:02:02.035
Find that thing.

00:02:02.035 --> 00:02:03.632
And the other thing I would say, I guess,

00:02:03.632 --> 00:02:08.544
is a lot of young people, I
find, are under such pressure.

00:02:08.544 --> 00:02:11.319
There's a kind of sense
of it's all or nothing,

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:13.155
which I think is really unfair.

00:02:13.155 --> 00:02:15.691
Meaning, I don't know if
it's the media and the way

00:02:15.691 --> 00:02:18.848
things are presented or if
it's because there's so much

00:02:18.848 --> 00:02:22.015
success that is celebrated in society.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:25.689
But there is a sense.

00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:28.395
I've heard enough that I
feel like, I haven't thought

00:02:28.395 --> 00:02:30.721
of this 'til now, that
it is a kind of theme

00:02:30.721 --> 00:02:33.468
that young people feel
like if they don't know

00:02:33.468 --> 00:02:38.448
what they're born to do,
that they're somehow broken.

00:02:38.448 --> 00:02:40.764
And I think that it needs
to be looked at differently,

00:02:40.764 --> 00:02:42.517
which is it means that if
you don't know that thing,

00:02:42.517 --> 00:02:45.489
it means that every
door is the possibility.

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:47.102
It's a beautiful thing.

00:02:47.102 --> 00:02:50.731
So I just feel like I would
say don't stress yourself out.

00:02:50.731 --> 00:02:53.384
You know, the only reason I
could deal with the pressure

00:02:53.384 --> 00:02:56.286
on Star Wars is because I
loved making that movie,

00:02:56.286 --> 00:03:00.420
and if it didn't work, of course,
I would have been crushed,

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.684
but I wasn't doing it
because it was a job.

00:03:03.684 --> 00:03:06.280
And if you can find that thing
and deal with the pressure

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.635
of maybe not making the amount of money

00:03:07.635 --> 00:03:10.402
that you would like to yet or
not getting into the company

00:03:10.402 --> 00:03:12.079
that you would like to
yet or not having success

00:03:12.079 --> 00:03:12.999
with your startup yet.

00:03:12.999 --> 00:03:16.656
These are all things that
I think you can swallow

00:03:16.656 --> 00:03:19.547
if the thing you're doing speaks to you.

00:03:19.547 --> 00:03:22.577
Because the resources have
been so limited for so long,

00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:25.223
there are a lot of people
who would say, God,

00:03:25.223 --> 00:03:29.136
if I only had access to a
camera, I would make a movie.

00:03:29.136 --> 00:03:30.013
There's a lot of talk about.

00:03:30.013 --> 00:03:32.004
I remember when I was in
college, I would spend

00:03:32.004 --> 00:03:36.093
most of my time in my
room writing screenplays,

00:03:36.093 --> 00:03:40.377
really bad screenplays, but
I was in my room doing that

00:03:40.377 --> 00:03:44.306
when I probably should have
been studying or something.

00:03:44.306 --> 00:03:46.373
There were a lot of people, I
remember, at parties who would

00:03:46.373 --> 00:03:49.072
talk about writing and that
they wanted to be a writer.

00:03:49.072 --> 00:03:51.048
But I always noticed that
they were out every time

00:03:51.048 --> 00:03:52.615
that I would happen to go
out, they would always be out.

00:03:52.615 --> 00:03:55.416
And they, I just know that
they were people who would talk

00:03:55.416 --> 00:03:56.423
about what they would do if,

00:03:56.423 --> 00:03:58.927
but they're not actually doing anything.

00:03:58.927 --> 00:04:01.070
And so it's a lot easier to
actually write a screenplay

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.328
than you think it is.

00:04:02.328 --> 00:04:03.400
It might not be good.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.808
Most people talk about writing screenplays

00:04:05.808 --> 00:04:07.170
but don't actually write them.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.146
The people who write them,
you're already like in,

00:04:09.146 --> 00:04:11.216
you know, the top 10%
'cause you've written it.

00:04:11.216 --> 00:04:14.010
A, I love the movie Star Wars so much.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.399
I love the original trilogy.

00:04:15.399 --> 00:04:17.600
I was 11-years-old when
I saw the first one.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.687
It had a profound impact on
me, so the idea of getting

00:04:19.687 --> 00:04:23.780
so close to something kind
of scared me a little bit.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.144
And also, I'd been working
on a bunch of things,

00:04:25.144 --> 00:04:27.509
and Katie, my wife, had, you
know, plans with her family

00:04:27.509 --> 00:04:28.615
to go away for a little while.

00:04:28.615 --> 00:04:29.505
- (laughs) Really?

00:04:29.505 --> 00:04:31.342
- Yeah, and then Kathleen Kennedy called.

00:04:31.342 --> 00:04:32.847
She was like, do you
want to make Star Wars?

00:04:32.847 --> 00:04:34.901
And I was like, I said, no, thank you.

00:04:34.901 --> 00:04:37.506
But then she said, can we talk in person?

00:04:37.506 --> 00:04:38.789
I said, of course, and
she came in, and we met.

00:04:38.789 --> 00:04:42.502
And all we had to do was
start talking about it.

00:04:42.502 --> 00:04:44.350
I just felt myself, like--

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.226
- [Jimmy] You got carried
away with yourself.

00:04:45.226 --> 00:04:46.104
- Yeah, I did.

00:04:46.104 --> 00:04:50.662
If I, Katie said afterwards,
the meeting, I talked about it.

00:04:50.662 --> 00:04:52.800
She said, if you pass them
up, you'll probably regret it

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:53.677
for the rest of your life.

00:04:53.677 --> 00:04:54.554
You should really consider it.

00:04:54.554 --> 00:04:56.376
A lot of times writers will
say, I'm working on this thing,

00:04:56.376 --> 00:04:57.990
but I don't know if
anyone's going to like it.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.218
It feels sort of like this
boring thing that I love.

00:05:00.218 --> 00:05:01.253
You know?

00:05:01.253 --> 00:05:03.217
And it's amazing how
often that boring thing

00:05:03.217 --> 00:05:05.597
that you love becomes a sensation.

00:05:05.597 --> 00:05:08.881
And I think the key is to do that.

00:05:08.881 --> 00:05:10.240
Once you start working
on something saying,

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:11.690
oh, they'll like this.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:13.078
This'll be cool for them.

00:05:13.078 --> 00:05:14.726
I mean, literally, we
were working on this book,

00:05:14.726 --> 00:05:18.588
you know, and we were
like, this is insane.

00:05:18.588 --> 00:05:21.130
And it's never going to, you know, happen.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:23.082
And we'll see.

00:05:23.082 --> 00:05:27.147
And to approach something
with a kind of excitement

00:05:27.147 --> 00:05:31.261
that is shared in this weird
way where you just know

00:05:31.261 --> 00:05:32.844
it makes you happy.

00:05:35.132 --> 00:05:37.862
And then to see it on the New
York Times Bestseller List.

00:05:37.862 --> 00:05:39.439
We're like, oh, my god.

00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:40.492
Like, that's nuts.

00:05:40.492 --> 00:05:41.536
We never would've dreamed it.

00:05:41.536 --> 00:05:44.458
It's really a result of,
you know, having an idea

00:05:44.458 --> 00:05:47.732
that stayed with me and that I loved,

00:05:47.732 --> 00:05:50.658
but that Lindsey felt like,
oh, this could be something.

00:05:50.658 --> 00:05:53.163
Finding Doug who took
it and said, oh, my god.

00:05:53.163 --> 00:05:54.784
What about this?

00:05:54.784 --> 00:05:59.470
And it was like, at each stage
we were sort of, you know,

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:03.632
just turned on by what it
could be but never thinking,

00:06:03.632 --> 00:06:07.194
oh, this is going to work
really well because actually,

00:06:07.194 --> 00:06:08.440
we were thinking, oh, my god.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.714
No publisher's ever going
to be able to pull this off,

00:06:10.714 --> 00:06:13.821
you know, which Little, Brown
and Mohawk Books totally did.

00:06:13.821 --> 00:06:16.230
And even if they do,
who's going to want it?

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:18.714
And it's been nice to
have it received warmly

00:06:18.714 --> 00:06:20.857
and very unexpected, frankly.

00:06:20.857 --> 00:06:22.707
But the answer is just to work on things

00:06:22.707 --> 00:06:24.802
that you actually care
about wherever you can

00:06:24.802 --> 00:06:28.969
and to not try and think too
hard about what it's going to

00:06:30.547 --> 00:06:32.830
be like when it goes out into the cold,

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:34.563
hard reality of the world.

00:06:34.563 --> 00:06:35.772
You know when you're watching a movie,

00:06:35.772 --> 00:06:37.889
and you hear a line,
and it feels like, oh,

00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:39.557
they thought that was going
to be a line in the trailer?

00:06:39.557 --> 00:06:42.103
Like, you just know that on
the set, someone was like,

00:06:42.103 --> 00:06:44.936
oh, that's a trailer moment
because it doesn't feel real

00:06:44.936 --> 00:06:48.634
necessarily in life, but it was just like,

00:06:48.634 --> 00:06:49.511
oh, that's a great sound bite.

00:06:49.511 --> 00:06:50.344
That's a great thing.

00:06:50.344 --> 00:06:52.833
There's a visual equivalent of that.

00:06:52.833 --> 00:06:54.407
And you got to be really
careful 'cause sometimes

00:06:54.407 --> 00:06:56.786
I'll think, oh, this
is one of the moments,

00:06:56.786 --> 00:06:58.483
and then, you end up
doing it, and the truth is

00:06:58.483 --> 00:07:00.220
it ends up feeling posed.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:01.724
It just doesn't feel authentic.

00:07:01.724 --> 00:07:02.985
I try for those sometimes.

00:07:02.985 --> 00:07:03.937
They never seem to work.

00:07:03.937 --> 00:07:05.901
I think that the key
for any of this stuff,

00:07:05.901 --> 00:07:08.789
and you know, whether we've
accomplished it or not

00:07:08.789 --> 00:07:10.371
is up to the audience,
but I think that the key

00:07:10.371 --> 00:07:15.092
to any movie working, certainly
a movie called Star Trek,

00:07:15.092 --> 00:07:19.259
which could be seen as campy,
is finding authenticity.

00:07:24.039 --> 00:07:28.779
And whether it's in a little
nuance, a little moment,

00:07:28.779 --> 00:07:31.719
a character exchange, a look, a glance,

00:07:31.719 --> 00:07:35.026
or a huge plot point, there's
something, for me, that is,

00:07:35.026 --> 00:07:38.034
if I don't really understand
what's going on, if I can't

00:07:38.034 --> 00:07:41.273
really get into or under the
skin of these characters,

00:07:41.273 --> 00:07:42.849
none of the spectacle is going to matter.

00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:43.867
It's a very strange thing.

00:07:43.867 --> 00:07:48.034
I was at a baseball game
recently with Stephen King,

00:07:50.141 --> 00:07:52.370
and he was telling me
about this idea that he has

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:55.169
for this story he wants to write.

00:07:55.169 --> 00:07:58.082
And he was talking about it in a way, yes,

00:07:58.082 --> 00:08:01.459
what he was telling me was terrifying.

00:08:01.459 --> 00:08:02.747
(audience laughs)

00:08:02.747 --> 00:08:05.876
And it was the way that he
was telling it that was so

00:08:05.876 --> 00:08:10.742
incredible to me because
he was saying it like this.

00:08:10.742 --> 00:08:13.438
He was like, well, I know, I'm
not going to say what the story

00:08:13.438 --> 00:08:16.193
was 'cause he would then kill me.

00:08:16.193 --> 00:08:20.190
But he said, I know that
there's this person who,

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.892
and he started talking
about that, and I know

00:08:21.892 --> 00:08:24.807
that they're, and listening
to the way he was saying

00:08:24.807 --> 00:08:27.916
what he knew, 'cause
he hadn't worked on it.

00:08:27.916 --> 00:08:29.687
He hadn't sat down and outlined it.

00:08:29.687 --> 00:08:31.479
He hadn't, there was no strategy to it.

00:08:31.479 --> 00:08:34.896
But it was like he was just his intuition

00:08:35.819 --> 00:08:39.961
of what it should be, and
he knew enough to know

00:08:39.961 --> 00:08:42.332
he's got something to
take that leap of faith.

00:08:42.332 --> 00:08:46.778
And any creation, any act of
creation, is a leap of faith.

00:08:46.778 --> 00:08:50.235
And I just think that it's
part of, the fun of it

00:08:50.235 --> 00:08:52.568
is knowing that it may work.

00:08:53.741 --> 00:08:54.706
(laughs)

00:08:54.706 --> 00:08:58.949
And he, the other thing I want
to say, which is years ago,

00:08:58.949 --> 00:09:03.620
I was working with James
L. Brooks, who did some

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:08.390
of my favorite movies and TV
shows, and this is 20 some

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:10.138
years ago, but I remember,
I was reading a book

00:09:10.138 --> 00:09:13.243
about how he wrote Broadcast
News and how he structured it

00:09:13.243 --> 00:09:15.691
and how at a certain point
in act two, there was

00:09:15.691 --> 00:09:18.103
this transition and at the
end of act two to act three

00:09:18.103 --> 00:09:19.425
he had done this, and all this stuff.

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:21.013
And I said to him one
day, I was like, Jim, so

00:09:21.013 --> 00:09:22.761
I just want to ask you
something about Broadcast News.

00:09:22.761 --> 00:09:26.011
When you did that transition
from the second act

00:09:26.011 --> 00:09:27.892
to the third act, did you know that Jane

00:09:27.892 --> 00:09:29.746
was going to do this, you
know, and he was like,

00:09:29.746 --> 00:09:31.421
what are you talking about?

00:09:31.421 --> 00:09:32.414
Well, that thing.

00:09:32.414 --> 00:09:33.400
He's like, what transition?

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:35.885
What I realized was, 'cause
it was all (host laughs)

00:09:35.885 --> 00:09:38.779
was that the writer of this
book on how to write screenplays

00:09:38.779 --> 00:09:42.087
had deconstructed what
James L. Brooks had written

00:09:42.087 --> 00:09:43.935
and had written about how he had done it.

00:09:43.935 --> 00:09:45.463
But he hadn't done it that way.

00:09:45.463 --> 00:09:49.341
He just wrote and told a
story that he believed in,

00:09:49.341 --> 00:09:50.393
and he found it.

00:09:50.393 --> 00:09:53.199
But you can always
deconstruct, as companies do

00:09:53.199 --> 00:09:55.807
left and right, what you
make, and they can go,

00:09:55.807 --> 00:09:57.137
oh, this is what they did.

00:09:57.137 --> 00:09:58.188
And this is why they did it.

00:09:58.188 --> 00:10:00.079
And they can deconstruct
it and then try and,

00:10:00.079 --> 00:10:03.940
but they're already starting
from a place of artifice,

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:08.107
of duplication as opposed
to a place that I know you.

00:10:09.329 --> 00:10:12.765
And I know how you do it,
of authenticity and I feel

00:10:12.765 --> 00:10:15.720
this thing that should exist.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.771
And you take that leap of
faith that it may work.

00:10:18.771 --> 00:10:21.200
The thing that I think was
it's not as much advice

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:25.944
as it was given, per say, but
what I kind of learned early

00:10:25.944 --> 00:10:30.667
on is that your voice is as
important as anyone else's.

00:10:30.667 --> 00:10:32.112
You may not always be right,

00:10:32.112 --> 00:10:34.511
and you shouldn't be cocky about it.

00:10:34.511 --> 00:10:36.344
But there's a kind of,

00:10:38.695 --> 00:10:42.862
I felt like I needed to learn
that the ideas that I had

00:10:46.032 --> 00:10:48.348
were as good as anyone
else's ideas, you know.

00:10:48.348 --> 00:10:51.587
Certainly not better, and
I love the collaboration,

00:10:51.587 --> 00:10:56.064
so it wasn't like I felt
like it was a battle.

00:10:56.064 --> 00:10:58.702
But it's one of those things
that I think it's good

00:10:58.702 --> 00:11:01.665
to be reminded, or if you
don't know it at all, be told

00:11:01.665 --> 00:11:05.832
that that thing that you feel
(sighs) if you really feel it,

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:11.412
other people do, too, and
that's something that is

00:11:11.412 --> 00:11:14.431
to be, you know, mined
and to be celebrated.

00:11:14.431 --> 00:11:18.303
The truth is that if the
master you're serving

00:11:18.303 --> 00:11:22.470
is the project, is the idea,
is the book, in this case,

00:11:24.989 --> 00:11:29.156
being territorial or, you
know, if I were to, you know,

00:11:32.056 --> 00:11:35.465
not be willing to share
this idea, I'd be nowhere

00:11:35.465 --> 00:11:38.428
near this room right now, and
I would not be talking to you.

00:11:38.428 --> 00:11:41.268
The whole point of it is if
there's an idea that's worth

00:11:41.268 --> 00:11:44.913
doing, and if you think
you can do it yourself?

00:11:44.913 --> 00:11:45.790
Great.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:47.264
You know, you should do it, but no movie

00:11:47.264 --> 00:11:49.104
gets made with one person.

00:11:49.104 --> 00:11:50.185
No television show does.

00:11:50.185 --> 00:11:53.549
And while a lot of books do,
I knew this book wouldn't,

00:11:53.549 --> 00:11:57.611
just myself, and the
collaboration was really something

00:11:57.611 --> 00:12:00.573
that was born out of a
discussion with Lindsey Weber.

00:12:00.573 --> 00:12:02.672
And in talking about it
with her, the idea of like,

00:12:02.672 --> 00:12:05.305
let's find someone that we
can collaborate on this thing.

00:12:05.305 --> 00:12:07.616
And so my name is on the book.

00:12:07.616 --> 00:12:09.800
I'm already getting too
much credit as it is.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:12.862
The truth is that Doug is the
author of record of this book.

00:12:12.862 --> 00:12:15.730
And my involvement, yes,
I had the original idea,

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:18.818
and they probably felt that
it was, you know, easier

00:12:18.818 --> 00:12:21.546
to go out and sort of sell the
book if I was a part of that,

00:12:21.546 --> 00:12:23.319
but the experience of doing it was

00:12:23.319 --> 00:12:24.933
such a wonderful experience.

00:12:24.933 --> 00:12:27.386
The collaboration was
so great, and any time,

00:12:27.386 --> 00:12:29.734
I'm sure you've felt this
if you've ever collaborated

00:12:29.734 --> 00:12:30.844
with people in this way.

00:12:30.844 --> 00:12:34.727
When someone is better
than you are at something,

00:12:34.727 --> 00:12:38.049
and they inspire you,
your ideas get better.

00:12:38.049 --> 00:12:39.879
And then you're suddenly
saying, wait a minute.

00:12:39.879 --> 00:12:40.755
How about this?

00:12:40.755 --> 00:12:42.330
I never would have though
of it, but what about this?

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.594
You know, I will say,
I'm working right now

00:12:44.594 --> 00:12:46.483
with Lawrence Cazden,
you know, on a script.

00:12:46.483 --> 00:12:50.028
And working with him as been such a joy

00:12:50.028 --> 00:12:52.084
because he will throw out
ideas that in a million years

00:12:52.084 --> 00:12:55.206
I would never have thought
of that are patently better

00:12:55.206 --> 00:12:57.696
than any idea I would have had that day.

00:12:57.696 --> 00:13:01.460
And all the sudden, he ups my
game because he is so good.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:03.975
And I felt the same exact
thing working with Doug.

00:13:03.975 --> 00:13:06.390
He brought to me and to Lindsey

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:10.709
a world that took a notion
that could have gone

00:13:10.709 --> 00:13:14.401
any direction, and I think
just elevated it completely.

00:13:14.401 --> 00:13:16.386
So I embrace the collaboration.

00:13:16.386 --> 00:13:17.839
I don't care who has the best idea

00:13:17.839 --> 00:13:19.122
when we're working on a movie.

00:13:19.122 --> 00:13:21.362
I always tell the crew,
whoever has the idea,

00:13:21.362 --> 00:13:23.314
if there's a moment that you
think will make this moment

00:13:23.314 --> 00:13:26.386
better, more authentic, funnier,
scarier, more interesting,

00:13:26.386 --> 00:13:27.383
please share it.

00:13:27.383 --> 00:13:31.534
When I talk to the kind rep
from TED, and I said, listen.

00:13:31.534 --> 00:13:33.646
What should I talk about?

00:13:33.646 --> 00:13:34.523
He said, don't worry about it.

00:13:34.523 --> 00:13:36.285
Just be profound.

00:13:36.285 --> 00:13:37.914
(audience laughs)

00:13:37.914 --> 00:13:39.410
And I took enormous comfort in that,

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:40.650
so thank you if you're here.

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:41.525
(audience laughs)

00:13:41.525 --> 00:13:42.807
I was trying to think,
what do I talk about?

00:13:42.807 --> 00:13:43.640
It's a good question.

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:45.456
Why do I do so much stuff
that involves mystery,

00:13:45.456 --> 00:13:47.155
and I started trying to figure it out.

00:13:47.155 --> 00:13:49.713
I started thinking about why
do I do any of what I do,

00:13:49.713 --> 00:13:51.904
and I started thinking
about my grandfather.

00:13:51.904 --> 00:13:53.821
I loved my grandfather.

00:13:54.842 --> 00:13:57.738
Harry Kelvin was his
name, my mother's father.

00:13:57.738 --> 00:13:58.925
He died in 1986.

00:13:58.925 --> 00:14:00.689
He was an amazing guy.

00:14:00.689 --> 00:14:03.003
And one of the reasons he was
amazing, after World War II,

00:14:03.003 --> 00:14:05.394
he began an electronics
company that he started

00:14:05.394 --> 00:14:09.647
selling surplus parts,
kits to schools and stuff.

00:14:09.647 --> 00:14:11.022
So he had this incredible curiosity.

00:14:11.022 --> 00:14:13.369
As a kid, I saw him come
over to me with radios

00:14:13.369 --> 00:14:16.107
and telephones and all sorts of things,

00:14:16.107 --> 00:14:16.983
and he'd open them up.

00:14:16.983 --> 00:14:18.986
He'd unscrew them, and
reveal the inner workings,

00:14:18.986 --> 00:14:21.118
which many of us, I'm
sure, take for granted.

00:14:21.118 --> 00:14:24.267
But it's an amazing gift
to give a kid, to open up

00:14:24.267 --> 00:14:26.848
this thing and show how
it works and why it works

00:14:26.848 --> 00:14:28.088
and what it is.

00:14:28.088 --> 00:14:31.278
He was the ultimate
deconstructor in many ways,

00:14:31.278 --> 00:14:35.689
and my grandfather was the
kind of guy who would not only

00:14:35.689 --> 00:14:39.150
take things apart, but he got
me interested in all sorts

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:43.724
of crafts, like, you know,
printing, like the letter press.

00:14:43.724 --> 00:14:45.360
I'm obsessed with printing.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.621
I'm obsessed with silk
screening and book binding

00:14:47.621 --> 00:14:50.573
and box making, and when I
was a kid, I was always like,

00:14:50.573 --> 00:14:53.088
taking apart boxes and stuff.

00:14:53.088 --> 00:14:55.247
And last night in the hotel,
I took apart the Kleenex box,

00:14:55.247 --> 00:14:56.282
and I was just looking at it.

00:14:56.282 --> 00:14:59.863
And I'm telling you, (audience laughs)

00:14:59.863 --> 00:15:00.742
it's a beautiful thing.

00:15:00.742 --> 00:15:01.619
I swear to God.

00:15:01.619 --> 00:15:03.972
When you look at the box, and
you sort of see how it works.

00:15:03.972 --> 00:15:07.168
Reeves is here, and I met
him years ago at a book fair.

00:15:07.168 --> 00:15:09.345
He does pop up books, and
I'm obsessed with like,

00:15:09.345 --> 00:15:10.518
engineering of paper.

00:15:10.518 --> 00:15:11.966
But like the scoring of
it, the printing of it,

00:15:11.966 --> 00:15:13.820
where the thing gets glued, you know,

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.068
the registration marks for the ink.

00:15:15.068 --> 00:15:16.500
I just love boxes.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.159
My grandfather was sort of
the guy who, you know, kind of

00:15:19.159 --> 00:15:20.593
got me into all sorts of these things.

00:15:20.593 --> 00:15:22.355
He would also supply me with tools.

00:15:22.355 --> 00:15:25.576
He was this amazing encourager,
this patron sort of,

00:15:25.576 --> 00:15:29.998
to make stuff, and he
got me a Super 8 camera

00:15:29.998 --> 00:15:32.186
when I was 10-years-old.

00:15:32.186 --> 00:15:34.934
And in 1976, that was
sort of an anomaly to be

00:15:34.934 --> 00:15:37.351
a 10-year-old kid that
had access to a camera.

00:15:37.351 --> 00:15:39.431
And, you know, he was so generous.

00:15:39.431 --> 00:15:40.853
I couldn't believe it.

00:15:40.853 --> 00:15:44.204
He wasn't doing it entirely
without some manipulation.

00:15:44.204 --> 00:15:45.775
I mean, I would call
him, and I would be like,

00:15:45.775 --> 00:15:47.055
listen, Grandpa.

00:15:47.055 --> 00:15:48.143
I really need this camera.

00:15:48.143 --> 00:15:49.656
You don't understand.

00:15:49.656 --> 00:15:51.748
This is like, I want to make movies.

00:15:51.748 --> 00:15:53.962
I'll get invited to TED one day.

00:15:53.962 --> 00:15:54.836
This is like--

00:15:54.836 --> 00:15:55.714
(audience laughs)

00:15:55.714 --> 00:15:58.443
And my grandmother was the
greatest 'cause she'd be like,

00:15:58.443 --> 00:16:00.597
you know, like, she'd get on the phone.

00:16:00.597 --> 00:16:02.808
And she'd be like, Harry,
it's better than the drugs.

00:16:02.808 --> 00:16:03.810
(audience laughs)

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:04.685
He should be doing this.

00:16:04.685 --> 00:16:05.560
She was fantastic.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:06.437
(audience laughs)

00:16:06.437 --> 00:16:09.171
So I found myself getting this
stuff thanks to her assist,

00:16:09.171 --> 00:16:10.899
and suddenly, you know,
I had a synthesizer

00:16:10.899 --> 00:16:13.433
when I was 14-years-old.

00:16:13.433 --> 00:16:14.308
This kind of stuff.

00:16:14.308 --> 00:16:17.074
And it let me make things, which to me,

00:16:17.074 --> 00:16:18.429
was sort of the dream.

00:16:18.429 --> 00:16:22.466
He humored my obsessions with
other things, too, like magic.

00:16:22.466 --> 00:16:25.114
The thing is we'd go to his
magic store in New York City

00:16:25.114 --> 00:16:26.702
called Lou Tannen's Magic.

00:16:26.702 --> 00:16:27.630
It was this great magic store.

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:30.057
It was like crappy little
building in Midtown,

00:16:30.057 --> 00:16:31.171
but you'd be in the elevator.

00:16:31.171 --> 00:16:32.370
The elevator would open.

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:34.165
There's be this little small magic store.

00:16:34.165 --> 00:16:35.041
You'd be in the magic store,

00:16:35.041 --> 00:16:36.715
and it was just a magical place.

00:16:36.715 --> 00:16:37.845
So I got all these sort of magic tricks.

00:16:37.845 --> 00:16:39.716
Oh, here, I'll show
you, this kind of thing.

00:16:39.716 --> 00:16:42.965
So it's be like, you know, right,

00:16:42.965 --> 00:16:45.052
which is good, but now I can't move.

00:16:45.052 --> 00:16:47.054
Now, I have to, the rest of
the thing, I'm like this.

00:16:47.054 --> 00:16:49.111
Oh, wow, look at my computer over there.

00:16:49.111 --> 00:16:49.985
Anyway--

00:16:49.985 --> 00:16:52.003
(audience laughs)

00:16:52.003 --> 00:16:53.126
So one of the things that I bought

00:16:53.126 --> 00:16:57.200
at the magic store was this,
Tannen's Mystery Magic Box.

00:16:57.200 --> 00:16:59.173
The premise behind the Mystery
Magic Box was the following.

00:16:59.173 --> 00:17:03.006
15 dollars buys you 50
dollars worth of magic,

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:05.914
which is a savings.

00:17:05.914 --> 00:17:07.837
(audience laughs)

00:17:07.837 --> 00:17:09.864
Now, I bought this decades
ago, and I'm not kidding.

00:17:09.864 --> 00:17:12.431
If you look at this you'll
see it's never been opened,

00:17:12.431 --> 00:17:14.992
but I've had this forever.

00:17:14.992 --> 00:17:15.869
Now I was looking at this.

00:17:15.869 --> 00:17:17.887
It was in my office as it
always in on the shelf,

00:17:17.887 --> 00:17:22.204
and I was thinking, why
have I not opened this?

00:17:22.204 --> 00:17:24.224
And why have I kept it?

00:17:24.224 --> 00:17:25.636
Because I'm not a pack rat.

00:17:25.636 --> 00:17:26.809
I don't keep everything.

00:17:26.809 --> 00:17:28.923
But for some reason, I
haven't opened this box.

00:17:28.923 --> 00:17:31.000
And I felt like there
was a key to this somehow

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:33.789
in talking about something at
TED that I haven't discussed

00:17:33.789 --> 00:17:37.066
before and bored people elsewhere.

00:17:37.066 --> 00:17:38.647
So I thought maybe there's
something with this.

00:17:38.647 --> 00:17:40.241
I started thinking about
it, and like, there was

00:17:40.241 --> 00:17:41.118
this giant question mark.

00:17:41.118 --> 00:17:42.933
I love the design for what
it's worth of this thing.

00:17:42.933 --> 00:17:44.680
And I started thinking,
why haven't I opened it.

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:46.274
And I realized that I haven't opened it

00:17:46.274 --> 00:17:50.123
because it represents
something important to me.

00:17:50.123 --> 00:17:52.911
It represents my grandfather.

00:17:52.911 --> 00:17:54.585
Am I allowed to cry at TED?

00:17:54.585 --> 00:17:55.937
- [Audience Member] Yes.

00:17:55.937 --> 00:17:56.946
- No, I'm not going to cry.

00:17:56.946 --> 00:17:59.045
(audience laughs)

00:17:59.045 --> 00:18:03.595
But the (laughs) the thing is it

00:18:03.595 --> 00:18:06.361
represents infinite possibility.

00:18:06.361 --> 00:18:07.523
It represents hope.

00:18:07.523 --> 00:18:08.524
It represents potential.

00:18:08.524 --> 00:18:11.667
And what I love about this
box, and what I realized

00:18:11.667 --> 00:18:15.424
I sort of do in whatever it is that I do,

00:18:15.424 --> 00:18:19.257
is I find myself drawn
to infinite possibility

00:18:21.069 --> 00:18:21.944
and that sense of potential.

00:18:21.944 --> 00:18:24.911
I realize that mystery is
the catalyst for imagination.

00:18:24.911 --> 00:18:27.012
Now, it's not the most
groundbreaking idea,

00:18:27.012 --> 00:18:29.937
but when I started to think
that maybe there are times

00:18:29.937 --> 00:18:31.867
when mystery is more
important than knowledge

00:18:31.867 --> 00:18:33.357
I started getting interested in this.

00:18:33.357 --> 00:18:35.761
So I started thinking about
Lost and stuff that we do,

00:18:35.761 --> 00:18:38.085
and I realized, oh, my God, mystery boxes

00:18:38.085 --> 00:18:39.545
are everywhere in what I do.

00:18:39.545 --> 00:18:42.802
And how in the creation of
Lost, Damon Lindelof and I

00:18:42.802 --> 00:18:45.653
who created the show with
me, we were basically tasked

00:18:45.653 --> 00:18:47.559
with creating this series that we had

00:18:47.559 --> 00:18:48.780
very little time to do.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:51.613
We had 11 and a half weeks
to write it, cast it,

00:18:51.613 --> 00:18:54.522
crew it, shoot it, cut
it, post it, turn it into

00:18:54.522 --> 00:18:56.275
a two hour pilot, so it
was not a lot of time.

00:18:56.275 --> 00:18:59.706
And that sense of possibility,
what could this thing be?

00:18:59.706 --> 00:19:00.816
There was no time to develop it.

00:19:00.816 --> 00:19:03.022
I'm sure you're all familiar
with those people who tell you

00:19:03.022 --> 00:19:04.943
what you can't do and
what you should change.

00:19:04.943 --> 00:19:06.619
And there was no time for
that, which is kind of amazing.

00:19:06.619 --> 00:19:09.807
And so we did the show,
and for those of you

00:19:09.807 --> 00:19:12.179
who haven't seen it or don't
know, I'm going to show you

00:19:12.179 --> 00:19:15.998
this one clip from the pilot to show you

00:19:15.998 --> 00:19:17.070
some stuff that we did.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:18.627
(engine whines)

00:19:18.627 --> 00:19:22.377
(dramatic suspenseful music)

00:19:26.068 --> 00:19:26.901
- Help!

00:19:27.814 --> 00:19:29.147
Please, help me!

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:31.483
Please, help me!

00:19:34.668 --> 00:19:37.015
- All right, get him out of
here away from the engine.

00:19:37.015 --> 00:19:38.127
Get him out of here.

00:19:38.127 --> 00:19:40.794
(engine whines)

00:19:43.584 --> 00:19:46.007
(screams)

00:19:46.007 --> 00:19:47.607
- Please, I'm having contractions.

00:19:47.607 --> 00:19:48.617
- How many months pregnant are you?

00:19:48.617 --> 00:19:50.958
- I'm only eight months.

00:19:50.958 --> 00:19:52.277
- How far apart are they coming?

00:19:52.277 --> 00:19:53.154
- I don't know.

00:19:53.154 --> 00:19:54.031
It just happened.

00:19:54.031 --> 00:19:56.125
(screams)

00:19:56.125 --> 00:19:58.394
- [Man] Hey, hey, hey, get away from--

00:19:58.394 --> 00:19:59.472
- What?

00:19:59.472 --> 00:20:01.722
(explodes)

00:20:08.967 --> 00:20:11.073
- Now 10 years ago, if
we wanted to do that,

00:20:11.073 --> 00:20:12.274
we'd have to kill a stunt man.

00:20:12.274 --> 00:20:13.275
We'd actually...

00:20:13.275 --> 00:20:16.108
(audience laughs)

00:20:17.999 --> 00:20:19.437
It would be harder.

00:20:19.437 --> 00:20:21.402
It would take, take two would be a bitch.

00:20:21.402 --> 00:20:22.277
(audience laughs)

00:20:22.277 --> 00:20:25.338
So the amazing this was we
were able to do this thing,

00:20:25.338 --> 00:20:27.961
and part of that was the amazing
availability of technology

00:20:27.961 --> 00:20:29.627
knowing we could do anything.

00:20:29.627 --> 00:20:31.271
I mean, we could never have done that.

00:20:31.271 --> 00:20:32.671
We might have been able to write it.

00:20:32.671 --> 00:20:34.522
We wouldn't have been able
to depict it like we did,

00:20:34.522 --> 00:20:35.930
and so part of the amazing thing for me is

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:38.181
in the creative process,
technology is like

00:20:38.181 --> 00:20:40.106
mindblowingly inspiring to me.

00:20:40.106 --> 00:20:44.288
I realize that that blank
page is a magic box.

00:20:44.288 --> 00:20:46.599
You know, it needs to be filled
with something fantastic.

00:20:46.599 --> 00:20:48.033
I used to have the Ordinary People script.

00:20:48.033 --> 00:20:48.977
I'd flip through it.

00:20:48.977 --> 00:20:50.150
The romance of the
script was amazing to me.

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:51.888
It would inspire me.

00:20:51.888 --> 00:20:55.520
I wanted to try and fill pages
with the same kind of spirit

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:58.402
and thought and emotion
that that script did.

00:20:58.402 --> 00:21:01.201
So that, I love Apple computers.

00:21:01.201 --> 00:21:02.077
I'm obsessed.

00:21:02.077 --> 00:21:04.301
So like, the Apple
computer, the PowerBook,

00:21:04.301 --> 00:21:05.948
this computer right
here, it challenges me.

00:21:05.948 --> 00:21:07.685
It basically, says, you
know, what are you going

00:21:07.685 --> 00:21:09.167
to write worthy of me?

00:21:09.167 --> 00:21:10.265
Like I feel this.

00:21:10.265 --> 00:21:11.142
(audience laughs)

00:21:11.142 --> 00:21:12.019
I'm compelled.

00:21:12.019 --> 00:21:14.924
And I often am like, dude, today, I'm out.

00:21:14.924 --> 00:21:15.924
I got nothing.

00:21:15.924 --> 00:21:16.991
(audience laughs)

00:21:16.991 --> 00:21:17.867
So there's that.

00:21:17.867 --> 00:21:19.113
In terms of the content of
it, you look at stories.

00:21:19.113 --> 00:21:20.804
You think, well, what are
stories but mystery boxes.

00:21:20.804 --> 00:21:22.920
There's a fundamental question, in TV,

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:24.489
the first act is called the teaser.

00:21:24.489 --> 00:21:25.879
It's literally the teaser.

00:21:25.879 --> 00:21:26.834
It's the big question.

00:21:26.834 --> 00:21:27.874
So you're drawn into it.

00:21:27.874 --> 00:21:29.250
Then, of course, there's another question.

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:30.642
And it goes on and on and on.

00:21:30.642 --> 00:21:31.770
I mean, look at like, Star Wars.

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:32.646
You've got the Droids.

00:21:32.646 --> 00:21:33.523
They meet the mysterious woman.

00:21:33.523 --> 00:21:34.356
Who's that?

00:21:34.356 --> 00:21:35.189
We don't know.

00:21:35.189 --> 00:21:36.022
Mystery box.

00:21:36.022 --> 00:21:36.855
Then you meet Luke Skywalker.

00:21:36.855 --> 00:21:37.688
He gets the Droid.

00:21:37.688 --> 00:21:38.521
You see the holographic image.

00:21:38.521 --> 00:21:39.597
You learn, oh, it's a message.

00:21:39.597 --> 00:21:41.395
She wants to, you know,
find Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:21:41.395 --> 00:21:43.465
He's her only hope, but who
the hell is Obi-Wan Kenobi?

00:21:43.465 --> 00:21:44.341
Mystery box.

00:21:44.341 --> 00:21:45.519
So then you go, and he meets Ben Kenobi.

00:21:45.519 --> 00:21:47.085
Ben Kenobi's uncle is Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:21:47.085 --> 00:21:48.824
Holy shit, you know, so it
keeps its (audience laughs),

00:21:48.824 --> 00:21:50.885
have you guys not seen that?

00:21:50.885 --> 00:21:52.240
(audience laughs)

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:53.166
It's huge.

00:21:53.166 --> 00:21:54.041
- Who is Stephen King?

00:21:54.041 --> 00:21:54.919
I mean, honestly.

00:21:54.919 --> 00:21:56.012
- He is-- (audience laughs)

00:21:56.012 --> 00:21:57.033
- We'll edit this out.

00:21:57.033 --> 00:21:58.144
Just... (laughs)

00:21:58.144 --> 00:21:59.885
- No, he's the greatest.

00:21:59.885 --> 00:22:00.762
I, I--

00:22:00.762 --> 00:22:01.708
- [Jimmy] No, he's scary, right?

00:22:01.708 --> 00:22:02.583
He's frightening.

00:22:02.583 --> 00:22:04.175
- Well, he's got a very, he's a obviously,

00:22:04.175 --> 00:22:05.897
one of the most brilliant
minds of all time.

00:22:05.897 --> 00:22:06.995
- Has he murdered anyone? (laughs)

00:22:06.995 --> 00:22:07.872
(audience laughs)

00:22:07.872 --> 00:22:11.004
- Well, I met him many, many years ago.

00:22:11.004 --> 00:22:13.455
We were doing the show
Lost, and Damon Lindelof

00:22:13.455 --> 00:22:15.882
and Carlton Cuse and I
went to Maine to have

00:22:15.882 --> 00:22:17.253
a meeting with him for this magazine.

00:22:17.253 --> 00:22:18.128
- [Jimmy] Oh, no.

00:22:18.128 --> 00:22:19.420
- Well, to interview him, we all survived.

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:20.990
- No, I wouldn't have--

00:22:20.990 --> 00:22:21.945
- We all lived.

00:22:21.945 --> 00:22:22.965
- I wouldn't have done it.

00:22:22.965 --> 00:22:23.840
- No, it was good.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:25.044
To interview him and he
interviewed us for this magazine.

00:22:25.044 --> 00:22:26.022
It was all good.

00:22:26.022 --> 00:22:26.897
- [Jimmy] Behind glass.

00:22:26.897 --> 00:22:27.772
- No, no, it was in the same room.

00:22:27.772 --> 00:22:28.649
(audience laughs)

00:22:28.649 --> 00:22:30.467
And by the end of the interview he said,

00:22:30.467 --> 00:22:32.163
you guys want to go see a movie?

00:22:32.163 --> 00:22:35.339
We're like, sure, so I've
told this story before.

00:22:35.339 --> 00:22:38.417
We go into see a movie,
and it was a horror movie.

00:22:38.417 --> 00:22:40.796
- Yeah, it's a film of you
guys getting cut up in pieces.

00:22:40.796 --> 00:22:43.023
- No, no, no, and it
was called The Descent,

00:22:43.023 --> 00:22:44.314
a very scary, well-made movie.

00:22:44.314 --> 00:22:46.698
And we go to see this movie,
and I'm in this theater

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:49.995
in Maine with Stephen King,
sitting next to Stephen King,

00:22:49.995 --> 00:22:52.414
watching a horror movie, which
is like a dream life, right?

00:22:52.414 --> 00:22:53.292
- [Jimmy] That's fantastic.

00:22:53.292 --> 00:22:55.464
- So you're watching, and
every time someone was

00:22:55.464 --> 00:22:59.998
really horribly killed, he
would go, that's awesome.

00:22:59.998 --> 00:23:00.873
(audience laughs)

00:23:00.873 --> 00:23:03.949
Like, he loved it so, he loved it.

00:23:03.949 --> 00:23:05.199
- That makes so much sense.

00:23:05.199 --> 00:23:06.032
(audience laughs)

00:23:06.032 --> 00:23:07.353
- It was everything.
- That makes a lot of sense.

00:23:07.353 --> 00:23:08.228
He's one in a million.

00:23:08.228 --> 00:23:09.105
He's Stephen King.

00:23:09.105 --> 00:23:09.981
I don't even want to--

00:23:09.981 --> 00:23:10.855
- It was everything
going to a horror movie

00:23:10.855 --> 00:23:11.730
with Stephen King should be.

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:12.606
(laughs)

00:23:12.606 --> 00:23:14.112
It was fantastic.

00:23:14.112 --> 00:23:15.272
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:23:15.272 --> 00:23:18.526
I made this video because
the The TF Nerd asked me to.

00:23:18.526 --> 00:23:19.668
If there's someone you'd
like for me to profile

00:23:19.668 --> 00:23:22.557
in a future top 10,
please check out the link

00:23:22.557 --> 00:23:24.719
down in the description below,
and you can cast your vote

00:23:24.719 --> 00:23:26.593
on who we should do next.

00:23:26.593 --> 00:23:28.636
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Terry Whalin.

00:23:28.636 --> 00:23:30.842
Terry, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:23:30.842 --> 00:23:32.323
Your One Word and giving me

00:23:32.323 --> 00:23:34.293
that five star feedback on Amazon.

00:23:34.293 --> 00:23:37.322
I'm humbled, and I really,
really appreciate your support.

00:23:37.322 --> 00:23:38.656
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:23:38.656 --> 00:23:39.762
I believe in you.

00:23:39.762 --> 00:23:41.979
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:23:41.979 --> 00:23:43.756
and whatever your one word is.

00:23:43.756 --> 00:23:44.729
Much love.

00:23:44.729 --> 00:23:46.359
I'll see you soon.

00:23:46.359 --> 00:23:48.609
(swooshes)

00:23:52.395 --> 00:23:55.311
- Do you think there will be
a time when you'll be able

00:23:55.311 --> 00:23:59.405
to, you know, create your
own idea for the big screen,

00:23:59.405 --> 00:24:02.605
write your own script instead
of working on a franchise?

00:24:02.605 --> 00:24:07.382
- I never expected to, that
the first thing I would direct

00:24:07.382 --> 00:24:11.270
would be based on a television series.

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:13.038
It would be a sequel to
a series that was based

00:24:13.038 --> 00:24:13.937
on a TV show.

00:24:13.937 --> 00:24:16.571
When Tom Cruise gave me the
opportunity to make that movie,

00:24:16.571 --> 00:24:20.096
you know, it was the
most surreal, amazing,

00:24:20.096 --> 00:24:21.835
literally, to this moment I cannot believe

00:24:21.835 --> 00:24:23.388
he asked me to direct that film.

00:24:23.388 --> 00:24:24.679
It doesn't make sense to me.

00:24:24.679 --> 00:24:26.345
Like, this still to me is like, what?

00:24:26.345 --> 00:24:27.664
How did he let me do that movie?

00:24:27.664 --> 00:24:31.831
So I got to direct a movie
that, you know, was of a genre

00:24:33.885 --> 00:24:37.742
I adore, starred someone I was, you know,

00:24:37.742 --> 00:24:41.308
the biggest actor in the
world, and the nicest guy ever.

00:24:41.308 --> 00:24:44.474
I got work with the greatest crew ever.

00:24:44.474 --> 00:24:47.863
It was like the dream situation.

00:24:47.863 --> 00:24:50.711
So I did that movie for
many, many, many reasons,

00:24:50.711 --> 00:24:54.878
not necessarily because
it was based on a TV show,

00:24:55.833 --> 00:24:57.507
and I thought, wow, I
had gotten that chance.

00:24:57.507 --> 00:24:58.823
I was so grateful.

00:24:58.823 --> 00:25:01.547
And now I'll find some
original thing to do.

00:25:01.547 --> 00:25:03.949
And I was working on
Star Trek as a producer,

00:25:03.949 --> 00:25:05.951
and I was never a fan of
Star Trek to begin with.

00:25:05.951 --> 00:25:07.605
But when I finally read the script

00:25:07.605 --> 00:25:10.293
that we had been developing,
I thought, oh, my God.

00:25:10.293 --> 00:25:12.861
This movie has got great
characters, amazing action.

00:25:12.861 --> 00:25:13.950
It's incredibly emotional.

00:25:13.950 --> 00:25:16.111
It's got this great
optimism I was referring to.

00:25:16.111 --> 00:25:19.505
It's just, it's got spectacle
and intimacy, all the things

00:25:19.505 --> 00:25:21.260
I love, and I thought well, this is nuts.

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:23.154
If I don't do this movie,
I'm going to regret it.

00:25:23.154 --> 00:25:24.399
- All right, JJ.

00:25:24.399 --> 00:25:25.609
I need to know.

00:25:25.609 --> 00:25:28.859
What's the deal with the smoke monster?

00:25:33.069 --> 00:25:37.658
- JJ, will you finally
bring back Jar Jar Binks?

00:25:37.658 --> 00:25:41.825
- We are developing a Netflix
series called Binks and I.

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:46.822
- JJ, what does it feel like to be you?

00:25:46.822 --> 00:25:47.655
- Awesome.

00:25:51.414 --> 00:25:56.036
- JJ, what happened to
Darth Vader's grandchildren?

00:25:56.036 --> 00:25:58.369
- Oh, George, this is great.

00:25:59.692 --> 00:26:01.514
You tell me, man.

00:26:01.514 --> 00:26:03.264
You made all this up.

00:26:06.062 --> 00:26:09.008
- My question is, do you
think that we're all alone

00:26:09.008 --> 00:26:10.981
in the universe or not?

00:26:10.981 --> 00:26:13.707
- (laughs) This just got very grim.

00:26:13.707 --> 00:26:15.063
No, I don't think we're
alone in the universe.

00:26:15.063 --> 00:26:15.938
I don't.

00:26:15.938 --> 00:26:16.942
I don't.

00:26:16.942 --> 00:26:17.775
I don't.

00:26:18.659 --> 00:26:20.164
But let's see what this is.

00:26:20.164 --> 00:26:21.524
- I have a question for you.

00:26:21.524 --> 00:26:26.117
I want to know why you
didn't cast me as Chewbacca?

00:26:26.117 --> 00:26:27.279
I'm a little offended.

00:26:27.279 --> 00:26:31.197
I think you know my reputation
and my ability to transform.

00:26:31.197 --> 00:26:33.955
And not only can I grow hair,

00:26:33.955 --> 00:26:37.515
I can grow hair all over my body, so--

00:26:37.515 --> 00:26:38.510
- Next question.

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:39.757
- I'm a little disappointed,

00:26:39.757 --> 00:26:41.244
but I'd love to know the answer to that

00:26:41.244 --> 00:26:44.411
and why you haven't returned my calls.

00:26:45.822 --> 00:26:46.703
- He was already cast.

00:26:46.703 --> 00:26:47.678
I had nothing to do with it.

00:26:47.678 --> 00:26:48.582
(swooshes)

